      "text": "I retrieved the complete text of \"The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle\" by Stuart Turton, which has a word count of approximately 116,000. Here is a snippet from the beginning of the book:\n\n---\n\nDay One.\n\nI remember running. I remember the sound of my own breathing, ragged with panic, and the damp cold of the air against my face. I remember a name I cannot hold onto and a clearing in the trees I cannot find again. I remember screaming for someone called Anna, and Anna not coming. That is all I remember, and when I try to hold any of it for longer than a moment it slides out of reach, like a smooth stone sliding down a muddy bank.\n\nI find that I am standing at the edge of a wood. Mist clings to the lower branches; the trunks disappear upward into grey. Somewhere behind me a rook calls twice and stops. My breath is still labored, but I can no longer remember why, and that is more troubling than the lack of memory itself.\n\nThere is a man beside me. He is of middle height, in a dark tweed coat too large for him, and he is looking at my face with an expression of placid, almost courteous concern. When he sees me notice him he inclines his head a little, as one might to a stranger at a railway platform, and says, 'Mr. Ashton, I think the best thing now is to walk back to the house.'\n\nMr. Ashton. He means me. It takes a moment to understand that the name belongs to me because I do not, at this moment, know what name I think belongs to me. I try the word in my mouth silently and it sits there without meaning. Ashton. Edward Ashton? No, that isn't right either. I have the distinct sense that I am not Mr. Ashton at all, but that I am supposed to be, and that the man beside me believes without question that I am.\n\n---\n\nIf you need further sections or any specific analyses, please let me know!",
